#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175707 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca7dee-1a1b-5af6-8719-5d2377004be9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Displacement of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "f2597771-4f5e-5a90-9c39-d8b902690340" :question/math-academy-id "e-568" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = 2t + 3t^2.$ What is the total displacement of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=3?$" :question/worked-solution "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={({t}^{2}+{t}^{3})|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[{3}^{2}+{3}^{3}]-[{1}^{2}+{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =[9+27]-[1+1] \\\\  & =36-2 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=3$ is $34.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "15fea8a5-1937-5f81-80f3-3bc88a51bec6" :question/math-academy-id "e-7128" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t) = 6t^2 +4t.$ At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-20.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=4?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in[2,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4) = x(2) + \\int_2^4 v(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(4) & =-20+{\\int }_{2}^{4}(6{t}^{2}+4t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =-20+{[2{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-20+([2(4)^{3}+2(4)^{2}]-[2(2)^{3}+2(2)^{2}]) \\\\  & =-20+([128+32]-[16+8]) \\\\  & =-20+(160-24) \\\\  & =-20+136 \\\\  & =116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=116.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175720 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "ac3a9d8a-a877-574d-86ef-be3ca3f5b61e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7129" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/assets/900988cb8ec54abcafc256a34c685829d2f5f00cdca373ad84dfa0ef70cc3957.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-1.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=8?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t = 8$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,8].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(8) = x(2)+ \\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=-1$, and we can compute the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=8.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt = 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(8) = -1+ 6 = 5\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=8$ is $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb5b1566-17c3-58b8-b00f-6cda1cf93271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292175} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb5ec3c-ac4f-533f-81f2-1fdb1ed88d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292176} {:answer/id #uuid "5332815c-36c3-58d9-991f-722f4ba2c53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292177} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1036c0-52e5-5d1c-8c38-60f8e5543902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292178} {:answer/id #uuid "92a786cd-fd48-5d59-bc9a-24358582507e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5332815c-36c3-58d9-991f-722f4ba2c53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292177} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea03fbc-b338-53c9-a25a-30131b5aa091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1052200-d76a-59c5-989a-4d2d76d89a0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-121007" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ At time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ the particle is located at the position $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+([-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3})]-[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5721c4-0839-5a8a-a15e-557f8f093ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313643} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfcb8cf-f802-53ff-9d62-565b626890b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313644} {:answer/id #uuid "1e50e90d-9111-5657-8aff-bd40208e73b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313645} {:answer/id #uuid "688a1453-a9b9-571d-8546-816af10d56a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313646} {:answer/id #uuid "c280a578-f923-5525-ada4-213a65a2d528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e50e90d-9111-5657-8aff-bd40208e73b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313645} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3ec097a-8980-5948-8de2-37f5956f9af5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3f4668a-4edd-5293-8ac3-235d3b4b17f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-121011" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/assets/18904539463b95f9d681f61f0d8f875ad0cb03b35d4037e75c227d4523502193.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=5?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=5$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,5].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(5)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=2,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=5.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(5)=2+6=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=5$ is $x=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1262e9c-17e1-531c-a52d-655bfc9701fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313650} {:answer/id #uuid "a8162d4f-3175-5cc0-8fcb-2d68bdb61a26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313651} {:answer/id #uuid "eadce465-34de-5311-9017-91847f2729f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313652} {:answer/id #uuid "18cf1192-fe57-58a9-8a63-498819a885a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313653} {:answer/id #uuid "07711a95-1b82-5563-8ec7-c2e467cea7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07711a95-1b82-5563-8ec7-c2e467cea7aa" :answer/type {:db/iden